WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:03.300
- In 2020, Egyptologists
from the University of Bonn

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:07.080
deciphered the oldest known
place name sign in the world,

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:10.140
dating back to the late
fourth millennium B.C.

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:12.360
The four hieroglyphs inscribed on a rock

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:15.030
in the Wadi el Malik east of Aswan, Egypt,

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:18.540
declared the region the Domain
of the Horus King Scorpion.

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:22.020
Of course, place names,
otherwise known as toponyms,

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:24.570
existed far before the Scorpion King,

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:27.540
both the 2002 and the 3070 B.C. versions.

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:29.910
But where do place names come from?

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:32.400
Well, toponymy reveals a lot

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:34.530
about the culture and history of a place,

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:36.270
including the people who lived there,

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:37.620
the nature of the settlements,

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:39.690
and the geographic landscape.

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:41.040
So that's your short answer.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:42.180
For the long answer,

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:45.060
including the longest
place names in the world,

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:48.480
join me as we dig into the
exciting world of toponyms.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:51.372
I'm Dr. Erica Brozovsky,
and this is Otherwords.

00:00:51.372 --> 00:00:54.289
(mysterious music)

00:00:58.592 --> 00:01:00.592
- [Narrator] Otherwords.

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:04.770
- I love superlatives more than anyone.

00:01:04.770 --> 00:01:06.390
So the first thing I wondered was

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:08.130
what's the longest toponym?

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:11.100
Growing up, I thought it
had to be the nearby lake,

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:12.840
commonly called Webster Lake,

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:14.010
but officially named

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:17.520
Lake
Chargoggagoggmanchauggauggagoggchaubunagungamaugg.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:19.530
The name comes from the Algonquin language

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:22.470
spoken by the Nipmuc people
indigenous to the area

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:25.350
and is said to mean
lake divided by islands

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:28.110
or fishing place at the boundaries.

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:30.990
At 45 letters, it's quite a mouthful,

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:33.810
but while it's the longest
place name in the US,

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:36.540
and the longest lake name in the world,

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:40.140
there are a few other places
that have it completely beat.

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:42.240
According to Guinness World Records,

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:45.720
Thailand's capital holds
the crown for longest name.

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:48.120
You might be thinking, "What, Bangkok?

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:50.100
That's shorter than Los Angeles!"

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:52.890
Well, Bangkok is actually
a colloquial name,

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:55.230
most popular with foreign visitors.

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:57.330
The locals usually call it Krung Thep,

00:01:57.330 --> 00:01:59.280
which means city of angels,

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:02.285
and is a shortening of the
official ceremonial name,

00:02:02.285 --> 00:02:07.285
Krung Thep Mahanakhon Amon
Rattanakosin Mahinthara Yuthaya

00:02:07.945 --> 00:02:12.945
Mahadilok Phop Noppharat
Ratchathani Burirom Udomratchaniwet

00:02:13.033 --> 00:02:17.031
Mahasathan Amon Piman Awatan Sathit

00:02:17.031 --> 00:02:19.260
Sakkathattiya Witsanukam Prasit.

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:22.980
In English, that's "city of
angels, great city of immortals,

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:26.100
magnificent city of the
nine gems, seat of the king,

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:29.190
city of royal palaces,
home of gods incarnate,

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:31.860
erect by Vishvakarman at Indra's behest."

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:34.230
But that's several words, isn't it?

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:36.360
To find the longest one word place name,

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:38.640
let's head further south to New Zealand,

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:40.834
where there's a hill the Maori have named

00:02:40.834 --> 00:02:43.905
Taumatawhakatangihangakoauauotamateaturipu-

00:02:43.905 --> 00:02:46.590
kakapikimaungahoronukupokaiwhenuakitanatahu,

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:48.870
which means the place where Tamatea,

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:50.100
the man with the big knees,

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:52.830
who slid, climbed, and
swallowed mountains,

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:54.060
known as landeater,

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:56.520
played his nose flute to his loved one.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:58.260
Not to be confused with a nearby hill

00:02:58.260 --> 00:02:59.910
where Tamatea, the man with the big knees,

00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:01.620
who slid, climbed, and
swallowed mountains,

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:02.453
known as landeater,

00:03:02.453 --> 00:03:04.590
played his accordion to his loved one.

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:06.090
Just kidding, I made that up.

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:08.580
If those are the longest place names,

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:09.840
what are the shortest?

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:12.090
Three letters? Two?

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:13.200
Believe it or not,

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.690
there are more than 40 places worldwide

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:18.060
named with just one letter.

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:21.690
You've got your A, E, I, O, U, and Y,

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:23.310
with various diacritics,

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:27.960
but also D, G, H, L, and W.

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:29.490
Hey, easy to spell.

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:33.240
Then I wondered what's the
most common place name?

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:35.760
According to the US Geological Survey,

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:38.490
Fairview and Midway are competing

00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:40.110
for the most common place name

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:41.280
in the United States,

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:45.000
with 288 and 256 locations, respectively.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.790
But neither comes close in terms of number

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:50.460
to the winner of the title: San Jose,

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:54.120
with more than 1,700 locations worldwide.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:55.470
The name is so common

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:57.960
likely because many of
the cities and towns

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:00.450
were founded by Spanish
speaking missionaries,

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:03.090
who chose the name St.
Joseph for his significance

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:04.200
to their faith.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:07.440
In fact, the top nine most common toponyms

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:08.790
were named after saints.

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:11.310
History is built into place names.

00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:13.920
Sometimes, it's as clear as a new name

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:15.780
because those who thought
they were discovering

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:19.320
slash colonizing lands
wanted a reminder back home,

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:22.500
hence, New Caledonia,
New Zealand, New Orleans,

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:23.940
and Nova Scotia.

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:25.380
There's also been a tradition

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:27.810
of naming these
questionably acquired lands

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:29.730
after colonial monarchs,

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:32.430
which is where we get all the
Georgetowns and Victorias.

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:35.760
Recently, movements to
throw off colonial names

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:39.330
and reclaim either indigenous
ones or new non-colonial ones

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:40.800
have become popular.

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:44.760
Limbe, Cameroon, for example,
was formerly called Victoria,

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:46.380
and there's been a political push

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:49.350
to add Aotearoa to New
Zealand's official name.

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:51.840
Interestingly, despite shifts in power,

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:53.730
due to say, invasion,

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:55.560
toponyms tend to be conserved,

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:58.740
surviving repeated language shifts.

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:00.810
The United States is full of places

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:03.060
that kept their Native American names,

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:06.090
even as the people who
named them were forced out.

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:07.710
In all over Western Europe,

00:05:07.710 --> 00:05:09.330
we see cities with Celtic names,

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:13.620
like London, Paris, Vienna,
Milan, Ghent, and Zurich,

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:14.730
none of which are home

00:05:14.730 --> 00:05:17.010
to the few surviving Celtic languages.

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:20.490
Even more specific history
can be baked into toponyms.

00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:21.540
In the British Isles,

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:23.160
you can deduce a fair amount

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:26.340
about a location settlement
demographics from its name.

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:30.210
For example, Sussex is the
land of the South Saxons,

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:33.120
Buckingham is the home belonging to Bucca,

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:37.560
and Canterbury is the fortified
town of the people of Kent.

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:39.030
This type of naming tradition

00:05:39.030 --> 00:05:41.670
based on residents is far from extinct.

00:05:41.670 --> 00:05:43.020
Think ethnic enclaves,

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:46.290
like Little Mogadishu,
Tehrangeles, or Basque Block,

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:48.210
that are named after the global locales

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:49.950
that the residents immigrated from.

00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:52.380
Native American place names may describe

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:54.720
not only local topographical knowledge,

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:57.240
but also information
about resources available

00:05:57.240 --> 00:05:58.770
at certain times of the year.

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:02.310
Manhattan is derived from
the Munsee word, manahahtaan,

00:06:02.310 --> 00:06:05.430
meaning place for gathering
the wood to make bows.

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:06.690
Massachusetts is derived

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:09.150
from the Wopanaak word, muswachasut,

00:06:09.150 --> 00:06:12.510
meaning near the great hill
or at the range of hills,

00:06:12.510 --> 00:06:13.860
and Mississippi is derived

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:15.930
from the Ojibwe word, misi-ziibi,

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:17.460
meaning great river.

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:20.070
Occasionally, a place name no longer

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:21.630
reflects the current landscape.

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:25.620
Back Bay in Boston is a bustling
metropolitan neighborhood

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:29.430
but has the name of a
water feature, curious.

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:31.140
Well, history tells us

00:06:31.140 --> 00:06:32.520
that it used to be a bay

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:34.770
until it was filled in in the mid-1800s,

00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:36.450
but the name stuck.

00:06:36.450 --> 00:06:37.920
What about Wall Street?

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:38.880
The actual street,

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:42.270
not the metonym for big
business or financial markets.

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:45.870
Wall Street in New York City
has a bull and fancy buildings,

00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:47.820
but no wall.

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:51.840
The street was named for a
defensive wall built in 1653

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:55.050
that was removed less than 50 years later,

00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:57.510
yet somehow that legacy still lasts.

00:06:57.510 --> 00:06:59.160
Inspiration for toponyms can come

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:02.850
from history, myth, climate,
geography, and more.

00:07:02.850 --> 00:07:04.350
But most important,

00:07:04.350 --> 00:07:06.540
they're a reflection of
our shared experience

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:08.523
on this planet called Earth.

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:12.330
Before you go, we're
looking for your feedback.

00:07:12.330 --> 00:07:16.110
Each year PBS Digital Studios
conducts an audience survey.

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:18.000
It helps us understand what you like

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:20.010
and what you wanna see more of.

00:07:20.010 --> 00:07:23.190
You also get to help PBS pick new shows.

00:07:23.190 --> 00:07:24.390
It only takes a few minutes,

00:07:24.390 --> 00:07:26.850
but your feedback is
extremely valuable to us.

00:07:26.850 --> 00:07:28.200
There's a link in the description.

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:29.794
Thanks in advance.

00:07:29.794 --> 00:07:32.310
Udomratchaniwet Mahasathan
Amon Piman Awatan Sathit

00:07:34.956 --> 00:07:36.933
Sakkathattiya Witsanukam Prasit.

00:07:38.730 --> 00:07:40.473
Yes!
- [Man] That was great.

00:07:40.473 --> 00:07:42.023
- (laughs) I'm happy with that.

